text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">p.150</span></p></td><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">Carlton Inn stables; Carlton Inn&nbsp;</span></p></td><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">(Man, Wm)&nbsp;</span></p></td><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">160 Leicester St, under Carlton</span></p></td></tr><tr style="height:0pt"><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">1900&nbsp;</span></p></td><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">p.184</span></p></td><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">Carlton Inn stables; Carlton Inn&nbsp;</span></p></td><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">(Volkel, Charles A)&nbsp;</span></p></td><td style="border-left:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-right:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-bottom:solid #000000 0.5pt;border-top:solid #000000 0.5pt;vertical-align:top;padding:0pt 5.4pt 0pt 5.4pt;overflow:hidden;overflow-wrap:break-word;"><p dir="ltr" style="line-height:1.2;text-align: justify;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:2pt;"><span style="font-size:10pt;font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;">160-162 Leicester St, under Carlton</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><br /></p><p><br /></p><p><span style="color: rgb(33, 33, 33); background-color: transparent;">PROV holds several records from the Carlton Inn. VPRS 7882 and&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">VPRS 7712 would be most useful, while VPRS 8350 gives a snapshot of what the hotel was like at the particular time.</span></p><p><br /></p><p><br /></p><h1>John Cozens</h1><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">The second licensee was John Cozens, from 1859 until his death in 1881. Cozens was born in Dorchester in England about 1820, the son of carpenter Thomas Cozens and his wife Mary. He was living with his parents at the time of the 1841 census, and his occupation then was listed as tailor. After his first wife died in 1846, John married Sarah Stark in </span><span style="color: rgb(38, 38, 38); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">Dorchester in November 1849. Sarah was born in South Leigh, Devon. At the 1851 census he is living with Sarah in Dorchester as a master tailor employing three apprentices. The Victorian gold discoveries likely brought the couple to Melbourne soon after, and they arrive on the ship </span><em style="color: rgb(38, 38, 38); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">Persia</em><span style="color: rgb(38, 38, 38); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"> in September 1852, his occupation recorded as tailor. It’s unclear whether the couple have any children before they emigrate. When they have son Thomas in Melbourne in 1855, they have already lost two sons, including Thomas William (born and died 1854 in Melbourne), and perhaps a child before they migrated. Two more of their children do not live beyond infancy —William Stark (died 1859), John Stark (died 1861 aged 4 months).</span></p><p><br /></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">In 1878 in the “Missing Persons” column of </span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">The Leader</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, John Cozens (Carlton Inn) sought information: “DORCHESTER — Wanted the address of SIDNEY HERBERT LOVELACE, son of Mr John Lovelace (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">The Leader</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 12 October 1878, 1)”, suggesting ongoing links to his English roots.</span></p><p><br /></p><p><span style="color: rgb(38, 38, 38); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">When John died aged 61 on 8 July 1881, his address was listed as </span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Southleigh Cottage, Dryburgh Street, Hotham Hill, the house named after Sarah’s birthplace (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Argus</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 9 Jul 1881, 1). Probate was granted to his wife Sarah Cozens and his only son Thomas Cozens of the same address (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Argus</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 14 July 1881, 8). His will is digitised at PROV, and he was buried in the Melbourne General Cemetery. Thomas married Ella Austin in Brighton in October that year (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Argus</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 7 October 1881, 1). Sarah died in 1890 aged 74 at Southleigh, Hampton Street, Brighton, her son Thomas listed as an ironmonger of Elizabeth Street (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Age</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 10 March 1890, 8). Her mother’s maiden name was Choron.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br /></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">At monthly land sales in November 1857, town lots were offered for sale in North Melbourne: “North of Grattan-street and the Carlton gardens, between Nicholson-street and the University Reserve, on the continuation westward of Johnston-street. Upset [reserve] price, £300 per acre”. John Cozens purchased Lot 20. One rood, £370 the lot” (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Argus</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 26 November 1857, 5). It’s likely that John and his family did not live at the hotel but in Dryburgh Street. In 1867, John Cozens was a judge of ales &amp; wines at the National Show on the day of the Royal Visit of Albert, the Prince of Wales (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Argus</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 29 November 1867, 6). He was a member of the Ancient Order of Foresters Court Friendship (</span><em style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Age</em><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">, 11 July 1881 4).</span></p>
        ]]></abstract>
